/.wordpress-org
/.git
/.github
/node_modules
/assets/scss

# The unminified tocbot build is kept in git as the vendored source of the file we
# do load, and left out of the ZIP: `Admin::initScripts()` enqueues `tocbot.min.js`
# and never `tocbot.js`, so those 45 KB were downloaded by every install and parsed
# by nobody. The minified file and tocbot's LICENSE both stay — the licence has to
# travel with the code.
#
# ⚠ The shape of this rule is not stylistic. The release builder decides whether a rule
# is anchored by testing for a leading slash, but strips the slashes a few lines earlier,
# so `anchored` is always false and a plain path rule only matches when one path segment
# equals it. `/assets/js/tocbot/tocbot.js` and `assets/js/tocbot/tocbot.js` both leave
# the file in the archive; the wildcard is what actually matches. Verified by running the
# builder, not by reading it. `tools/assets.php` mirrors the same matching for the same
# reason, and says so.
assets/*/tocbot/tocbot.js

# Source maps are not shipped. This is also one of the release builder's built-in
# exclusions, and it used to be left to that default — which is how two `sourceMappingURL`
# comments survived a build whose `.map` files were never in it. Declared here so that the
# rule is readable from the repository rather than inferred from a tool.
*.map

# Development-only files. None of them are needed at runtime, and shipping them
# costs the user download time and tells an attacker what the project's tooling
# is. `/vendor` is deliberately NOT here — WordPress.org installs never run
# `composer install`, so the release has to carry its own dependencies.
.distignore
/.editorconfig
/.gitignore
/.gitattributes
/deptrac.yaml
/phpcs.xml
/phpcs.xml.dist
/phpcs-advisory.xml
/phpcs-advisory.xml.dist
/phpstan.neon
/phpstan.neon.dist
/phpunit.xml
/phpunit.xml.dist
/plans
/tools
/tests
/docs
/dependabot.yml

  # Kept in the release on purpose, because they are for the person running the
  # site or writing an extension, not for the person building the plugin:
  #   security.txt — the security contact and the supported-versions table
  #
  # The files that used to be listed here — UPGRADE.md, EXTENDING.md, SECURITY.md,
  # SUPPORTED_VERSIONS.md, CHANGELOG.md — were removed on 30.09.2026. Their content
  # is not lost: the upgrade procedure and the changelog live in `readme.txt`
  # (== Upgrade Notice == and == Changelog ==), which is what wordpress.org renders,
  # and the security contact lives in `security.txt`. `EXTENDING.md` had no replacement
  # — the extension contract arrives with 0.11.0, so there is nothing to document yet.

# `composer.lock` is intentionally NOT excluded.
#
# It used to be, which made every released build's dependency tree
# unauditable: nobody could answer "which version of monolog is in the ZIP that
# 4 000 sites are running", and `composer audit` against a release was
# impossible. The lock file is the record of what actually shipped. The risk of
# publishing it — revealing the dependency list — is a risk the project already
# takes by shipping `vendor/` and `composer.json` in the same ZIP.
#
# The `dev-*` branch constraints in `require` make the lock file especially
# worth keeping: with a lock, an install is reproducible, and without one the
# branch head is whatever it happened to be on the day the ZIP was built.
